No assertion template is declared. The template that would otherwise apply, the published Project Delphi evidence item template https://w3id.org/np/RAk4kcX4zA_touv8CeKku9tTUnbR5xiTLSLwFbrKSl3BE (dct:created 2026-10-01), still carries the legacy https://w3id.org/probknow/ontology/ namespace (1 occurrence, checked 2026-10-31 on the live network), and its required statements cannot be instantiated with Delphi ontology 2.0 terms, so this hand-authored record was not created from it and does not claim to be. The provenance template https://w3id.org/np/RAaYeFYF-Cz2bM5S1wPQsnvs5X6BBxsphL03UkbVZ1lcA is declared because it carries no legacy IRI (0 occurrences, same check). No pubinfo template is declared. See claim-tests/nanopubs/DECISIONS.md (2026-10-31). Pre-registered test of the claim. Hypothesis as tested: If neutral equilibrium heterozygosity H = theta/(1+theta) with theta = 4*Ne*mu holds and effective size scales with census size across species, then genome-wide nucleotide diversity (pi, the per-site heterozygosity) across metazoan species rises with census population size N: for pi << 1, H is approximately 4*Ne*mu, so log10(pi) should increase with log10(N) with a slope near 1 when mu and Ne/N are comparable across species, and the rank correlation between pi and N should be strongly positive. Census size stands in for effective size and per-site mutation rate is not included, so the refutation is of the law as operationalized (diversity should track census size), which is exactly Lewontin's paradox: diversity does rise with N (rho about 0.5, p < 1e-10) but roughly ten times too slowly on a log scale, across 12 orders of magnitude of N. Census sizes are model-based estimates (species range times a body-size-predicted density) with large uncertainty; measurement error in N attenuates the slope, but not by the factor of ten needed to rescue the law (Buffalo 2021 reaches the same conclusion with error-in-variables models). Diversity estimates mix synonymous, non-coding and genome-wide measures from three collations, and are averaged where several entries exist for a species. Class Aves (13 species) was excluded before fitting at the holdout thread's request and can be scored later with 'analysis.py --exam'. The first run of the script, before the gate was added, printed the birds' mean residual once; it was not used to change anything. This is a cross-species pattern; it does not test the within-model derivation H = theta/(1+theta), which is a theorem. Post-hoc discriminating-power check (perturbation.py, added 2026-10-31 after the result; simulation, not observation): with the observed census sizes and residual scatter, the pre-registered rule returns 'supports' in 100% of 2000 simulated datasets when the true slope is 1.0 or 0.75 and in 97.6% at 0.5, 'mixed' in 97.6% at 0.25, and 'refutes' in 100% at slope 0; the observed slope of 0.107 returns 'refutes'. The refutation is therefore not a failure of test sensitivity.
